XI. Drone Surveillance: Privacy and Evidence Governance

This subsystem needs a distinct governance boundary because drone imagery can capture people, residences, private activities, and sensitive locations.

I recommend these non-negotiable design principles:

Principle	Implementation

Purpose limitation	Document why collection is necessary
Lawful authorization	Confirm permission before acquisition
Data minimization	Collect only what is needed
Geographical restriction	Enforce approved collection boundaries
Read-only evidence	Preserve original records
Privacy protection	Restrict unnecessary identification
Retention limitation	Apply documented retention schedules
Human oversight	Require review for consequential decisions
Auditability	Preserve access and processing history
Secure deletion	Dispose of information when retention expires


The system should not support covert tracking of private individuals, unauthorized collection, or automated identification and targeting.

For environmental monitoring, infrastructure inspection, disaster response, authorized land surveys, and public-interest research, the same architecture can provide useful data-integrity and accountability controls.


